Expected elements: present

All MUST SHOW items are on screen: the affirmative `Real Aztec testnet data` banner above the identity bar naming `aztec-testnet`, its endpoint, moment and the 63629–63678 replay window; an identity bar with a real hash, `block 63675`, both stepping directions, `128 / 345`, the phase rail and an exit that lands on the chain; instruction-level explanations in Code, Call Trace and Values; `Supply sources`; and replay telemetry with `effectsMismatched: 0`. Nothing forbidden: no invented source, no fixture content, no unexplained blank pane, no failure banner. The shell fits 1080 exactly — no page scroll, no overhang, no clipped band.

Structurally the layout is sound; what is wrong is how the space is allocated and how many left edges it uses.

Worst: the split gives 1523px of width to eight lines of prose while the 390px Transaction pane — the only pane holding data — is the only one whose content overflows. Second: the banner spends 200 of 1080 rows to set a 600px measure inside a 1912px card, leaving two-thirds of it empty in the register where rows are scarcest. Third: the fee row wraps to four lines and breaks the pane's label/value grid. Fourth: four different left edges down the page (11 / 14 / 20 / 23). Fifth: the active phase segment's fill does not match its container radius and reads as a stray glyph.

Highest-priority fixes: reflow the banner to its available width, and widen the Transaction column at this viewport.

Rating: 6/10

